// ListCheckOutAsks The check-out asks you may answer
//
// THE PENDING ASKS: "a second list on the same page, longest waiting first: who is asking,
// which key, why in one line, and for how long — answered with Approve or Refuse with a reason"
// (`answerCheckOut`). Each is a check-out in the state `asked`. One that was answered leaves
// this list: approved, it is on `listCheckOuts`; refused, it is on neither.
//
// "THE PENDING ASKS SHOW ONLY TO WHOEVER MAY ANSWER THEM." See the check-out asks you may answer
// (`keys:check-out-ask.read`) is given to every role that holds Approve somebody else's ask, and
// to whoever holds Approve a reveal without ever seeing the value, so a trusted person can find
// the ask they may answer. It is offered in a personal environment and a business one, and it
// can be given on one key or its key group. An ask sits inside its key, so the door asks no
// permission, and each row is judged by its key. An ask you may not see is absent, never masked.
//
// YOUR OWN ARE ALWAYS HERE. "A person always sees their own asks and their own check-outs, with
// or without it": an ask you made is listed whatever you were given. It is shown and never
// yours to answer: approving your own is refused inside `answerCheckOut`.
//
// THE LONGEST WAITING FIRST, by when each was asked for. The page said "worst first" until 26
// October 2026, and this contract read it this way; the spec lane confirmed the reading and the
// page now says it.
//
// A GUEST SHARE THAT NEEDS A SIGNATURE waits under the same PENDING ASKS on the page, and is
// read with `listGuestShareAsks`: it is answered under a permission of its own, given across
// the account environment, where a check-out's is given on a key. The page shows both lists'
// rows in one, longest waiting first.
//
// A PAGE SERVED IS ONE LOOK, an empty one too, and it names no ask.
//
// Corresponds with GET /check-out-asks (the `ListCheckOutAsks` operationId).
func (c *Client) ListCheckOutAsks(ctx context.Context, params *ListCheckOutAsksParams, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*http.Response, error) {
	req, err := NewListCheckOutAsksRequest(c.Server, params)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	req = req.WithContext(ctx)
	if err := c.applyEditors(ctx, req, reqEditors); err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return c.Client.Do(req)
}

// ListCheckOuts The check-outs you may see
//
// WHO HOLDS WHICH PRIVILEGED KEY, WHY AND UNTIL WHEN, and what happened when each came back.
// "One list, saved views — never tabs. The views are Open · History", so `view` says which,
// and a check-out moves from one to the other when it comes back.
//
// EACH CHECK-OUT IS ASKED ABOUT, BY ITS KEY, NEVER THE WHOLE ENVIRONMENT. See check-outs
// (`keys:check-out.read`) can be given on one key or its key group, and a check-out sits inside its
// key. So the door asks no permission, and each row is judged by its key: somebody given it on
// one key is answered that key's check-outs. A check-out you may not see is absent, never
// masked.
//
// YOUR OWN ARE ALWAYS HERE. "A person always sees their own asks and their own check-outs, with
// or without it": a check-out you hold, or held, is listed whatever you were given.
//
// ONLY CHECK-OUTS THAT RAN. An ask nobody has answered, and one that was refused, never held a
// key, and they are not on this list: "PENDING ASKS stays its own thing, because a request is a
// different object." The asks nobody has answered are `listCheckOutAsks`; a refused one is on
// neither list.
//
// OPEN is every check-out still out: held, coming back, or back with its value still live.
// HISTORY is every one that is over. A check-out whose time is up is over, and is in History
// before the returner has written it down.
//
// NEWEST FIRST, in both views, by when it was asked for. "The row stays at the top of Open until
// somebody rotates it by hand or accepts the risk" is not built: nothing tries a rotation yet,
// so no check-out is ever back with its value still live, and that order comes with the first
// rotation that can fail (ONPLO-1587). Accepting the risk is `acceptUnrotatedReturn`; until
// 8 November 2026 this said the order came with it.
//
// A PAGE SERVED IS ONE LOOK, an empty one too, and it names no check-out.
//
// Corresponds with GET /check-outs (the `ListCheckOuts` operationId).
func (c *Client) ListCheckOuts(ctx context.Context, params *ListCheckOutsParams, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*http.Response, error) {
	req, err := NewListCheckOutsRequest(c.Server, params)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	req = req.WithContext(ctx)
	if err := c.applyEditors(ctx, req, reqEditors); err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return c.Client.Do(req)
}

// GetCompanyKeyRules See the company key rules
//
// THE COMPANY'S FLOOR FOR ITS KEYS, ONE SET PER ACCOUNT ENVIRONMENT. A rule nobody has set
// answers its default, so this always answers every rule: a client never has to know which
// were touched.
//
// IT POINTS AT NO OBJECT. The rules are the environment's own and there is one set, so the
// question "may this caller see them" is asked of the whole environment (decision 0632).
//
// ONLY WHERE THERE IS A COMPANY SIDE: a business account environment, or Onplo's own team
// (decision 0663). In a personal one there are no company key rules to see, and the answer is
// a 404 with the code `environment_has_no_company_side`. Where the authorization service did
// not say which kind of account environment the request is in, it is
// `authorization_unavailable` — ask again — and never that 404: not known is not personal.
//
// A MEMBER NEEDS NO PERMISSION TO READ THE SENTENCE A RULE PUTS ON THEIR OWN KEY; that
// sentence travels with the key. This operation is the Key rules page.
//
// SEEING THE RULES IS RECORDED. Each look that is answered leaves one audit event — the rules
// its target, about nobody, marked a look and naming no change — as every look does, and not
// only a look at a secret (decision 0675). A look that was refused saw nothing and leaves none.
//
// Corresponds with GET /company-key-rules (the `GetCompanyKeyRules` operationId).
func (c *Client) GetCompanyKeyRules(ctx context.Context, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*http.Response, error) {
	req, err := NewGetCompanyKeyRulesRequest(c.Server)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	req = req.WithContext(ctx)
	if err := c.applyEditors(ctx, req, reqEditors); err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return c.Client.Do(req)
}

// UpdateCompanyKeyRulesWithBody Set the company key rules
//
// EVERY FIELD IS OPTIONAL, AND AN OMITTED RULE IS UNTOUCHED. The answer is the whole set as
// it now stands. Sent again, the same change changes nothing.
//
// A RULE FLAGS AND BLOCKS A NEW SAVE; IT NEVER REWRITES OR DELETES A KEY. Lowering how many
// previous values are kept trims nothing by itself: a key's history is trimmed to the new
// number the next time that key's value is rotated.
//
// ONLY WHERE THERE IS A COMPANY SIDE, as `getCompanyKeyRules` says, with the same two answers.
//
// ALWAYS PRIVILEGED IS SENT WHOLE, the kinds and the key groups each: a list replaces the one that
// stood, and the same kind or key group named twice is refused (422 `company_key_rules_malformed`).
// A list that says what stands already, in any order, changes nothing. EACH KEY GROUP NAMED IS ONE
// OF THE COMPANY'S in this account environment, asked of the vault before anything changes:
// a key group that is not there, or one of somebody's own, is refused with 422
// `always_privileged_ring_not_found`, and which of the two it was is not said.
//
// KEY GROUPS SHAREABLE TO GUESTS IS SENT WHOLE in the same way, each key group with whether an approver
// signs: the same key group named twice is refused (422 `company_key_rules_malformed`), and a key group
// that is not one of the company's is refused with 422 `guest_shareable_ring_not_found`.
//
// PERSONAL KEYS INSIDE THE COMPANY IS SENT WHOLE, both of its halves. Turned off, it stops
// new personal keys and key groups of one's own in the company, and the ones already there stay
// as they are.
//
// THE CHECK-OUT SETTINGS — Maximum duration and Reason required — hold for every key in
// Check-out in this account environment, never per key. They are read when somebody asks for
// a check-out (`requestCheckOut`), and a change reaches the next ask only.
//
// A CHANGE IS RECORDED WITH THE NUMBER BEFORE AND AFTER, the always-privileged kinds and
// key groups before and after, each with its floor, personal keys inside the company before and
// after, each check-out setting before and after, and the key groups shareable to guests before
// and after, each with whether an approver signs. One audit event for a change that
// really moved a rule — the rules its target, about nobody, marked a change — saying what the
// rule stood at and what it stands at. A save that moved nothing names no change and still
// shows the rules, so it is recorded as a look (decision 0685).
//
// MORE THAN TEN PREVIOUS VALUES IS PREMIUM'S (decision 0822): "FREE KEEPS TEN, PREMIUM KEEPS
// UP TO 100". Raising the number above ten, and above what it stands at, needs Keys' Premium
// seat held by the one making the change. Without it the change is refused with 409
// `not_in_plan`, saying what is not included, and nothing changes. A RULE ALREADY SET STANDS
// IF THAT SEAT LAPSES: fifty stays fifty, and every rotation keeps fifty. LOWERING IT NEVER
// NEEDS A SEAT, and neither does the number already held sent again. Up to ten, Account is
// not asked at all; above it, Account out of reach is 503 `account_unavailable` only where
// the seat was needed.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type.
//
// Corresponds with PATCH /company-key-rules (the `UpdateCompanyKeyRules` operationId).
func (c *Client) UpdateCompanyKeyRulesWithBody(ctx context.Context, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*http.Response, error) {
	req, err := NewUpdateCompanyKeyRulesRequestWithBody(c.Server, contentType, body)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	req = req.WithContext(ctx)
	if err := c.applyEditors(ctx, req, reqEditors); err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return c.Client.Do(req)
}

