// GetKeyHealth How your keys are doing, one group per finding
//
// KEY HEALTH'S GROUPS, WORST FIRST, EACH WITH ITS COUNT: "Exposed in a known breach · Reused ·
// Weak · Two-step available, not set up · Expiring or rotation overdue · Saved on an insecure
// address · Unused for longer than the dormancy window · Known by a leaver (company only)". The
// rows of a group are `listKeyHealthFindings`.
//
// A CHECK THAT DID NOT RUN IS NEVER ANSWERED AS A CLEAN BILL. Every group says whether its check
// ran: `checked`, with its count, or `not-checked-yet`, `did-not-run` or `switched-off`, with no
// count. "Nothing to do and Cannot tell are different answers, and this is the one page where
// confusing them is dangerous." So a group whose check cannot run yet is answered
// `not-checked-yet`, never 0.
//
// WHICH CHECKS KEYS MAKES ITSELF, AND WHICH IT CANNOT YET. Expiring or rotation overdue, and an
// insecure address, need no password and are Keys' own, worked out from each key at the moment
// of the read.
//
// EXPOSED, REUSED AND WEAK ARE WHAT A DEVICE FOUND (decision 1037): "a device that can open the
// entry runs them and sends Keys only the finding", through `recordKeyHealthDeviceFindings`.
// Each of the three is `checked` once a device has checked at least one key you see, and its
// `last_checked_at` is the OLDEST check among them, so the date never claims more than the
// stalest key. `unchecked_key_count` says how many of your keys no device has checked yet: a
// key with no finding is never counted as healthy. Weak is a band below `medium`, the Weak
// threshold's default. Reused is two or more keys you see with one owner and one reuse group.
//
// UNUSED COUNTS FROM THE VAULT'S LAST USE (ONPLO-5743): "The vault keeps only when each key was
// last used, because every use passes through it." Keys' health worker reads it from the vault for
// each key this read sees, within the hour, and again each day. Unused is `checked` once one key
// you see has been read, counted over the keys read, and its `last_checked_at` is the OLDEST
// read. `unchecked_key_count` says how many of your keys were not read yet: never counted as used,
// nor as unused. A key is unused when its last use, or, never used, the moment it was made, is
// more than 12 months before this read: Unused after's default. Two-step, which needs the site
// catalog, and Known by a leaver are `not-checked-yet`.
//
// ONLY WHAT YOU MAY SEE, as `listKeys` answers it. A key you may not see is in no count, absent
// and never masked, and a key in the Trash is in none. The Personal and Company chips are
// `owner_scope`; left out, both.
//
// THE LEAD TIME IS 30 DAYS FOR NOW. "Expiry lead time" is a setting of Key health with a default
// of 30 days, and Key health's settings have no home in Keys yet (ONPLO-1335): until they do,
// a key expiring within 30 days is listed, the Weak threshold is `medium`, Unused after is 12
// months, and every check is on.
//
// Corresponds with GET /key-health (the `GetKeyHealth` operationId).
func (c *Client) GetKeyHealth(ctx context.Context, params *GetKeyHealthParams, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*http.Response, error) {
	req, err := NewGetKeyHealthRequest(c.Server, params)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	req = req.WithContext(ctx)
	if err := c.applyEditors(ctx, req, reqEditors); err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return c.Client.Do(req)
}

// RecordKeyHealthDeviceFindingsWithBody What a device found when it checked keys it can open
//
// THE CHECKS THAT NEED A PASSWORD RUN ON A DEVICE, BECAUSE ONPLO CANNOT OPEN A KEY (decision
// 1037). "Exposed, Reused and Weak each need the value, so a device that can open the entry runs
// them and sends Keys only the finding." This is where it sends them: for each key, whether it
// is in a known breach, its reuse group, its strength band, and when the device checked it.
// Never a value, and never a hash of one.
//
// A REUSE GROUP IS AN ID THE DEVICE MAKES UP AT RANDOM, "shared by the keys of one owner that
// hold the same value, never across owners". It says nothing about the value. Keys holds two
// keys as reused only when both have the same owner and the same group, so a group id that
// turns up under two owners counts for neither.
//
// ONLY KEYS YOU MAY READ. Each key in `key_ids` is asked `keys:key.read` on its own
// (`x-each-named-requires`). One you may not read, or one that does not exist, is a 404, and
// nothing is recorded. A key in the Trash is `key_in_trash`.
//
// UP TO 200 KEYS A CALL, which is one permission check. Each key in `key_ids` has exactly one
// finding in `findings`, and each finding names a key in `key_ids`. A check dated more than
// five minutes ahead of Onplo's clock is refused: five minutes is a device's clock running fast.
//
// A NEWER FINDING STAYS. A finding replaces the one Keys holds only when its check is later.
// So a device that was offline and sends an old check later does not undo a newer one.
// `kept_newer_key_count` counts the keys whose finding stayed.
//
// ONE CHANGE IN THE AUDIT LOG PER KEY WHOSE FINDING WAS RECORDED, the key its target. The event
// says that the finding changed, never what was found.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type.
//
// Corresponds with POST /key-health/device-findings (the `RecordKeyHealthDeviceFindings` operationId).
func (c *Client) RecordKeyHealthDeviceFindingsWithBody(ctx context.Context, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*http.Response, error) {
	req, err := NewRecordKeyHealthDeviceFindingsRequestWithBody(c.Server, contentType, body)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	req = req.WithContext(ctx)
	if err := c.applyEditors(ctx, req, reqEditors); err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return c.Client.Do(req)
}

