// CreateKeyWithBody Add a key
//
// THE VALUE IS SENT ONCE, HERE, AND NEVER COMES BACK IN THIS ANSWER. What returns is the entry.
// A write-only value is typed once and masked for ever, unreadable even to the Onplo team; a
// readable one is read back through `revealKeyValue`, by somebody allowed to.
//
// READABILITY IS SAID, NEVER GUESSED. The screen prefills it by kind — everyday kinds readable;
// `api-key`, `ssh-key` and `machine-secret` write-only — and the entry table states no default
// for a certificate or encryption key. Whether a secret can ever be read back is the one fact
// an API must not assume for a caller, so `readability` is required here and a client carries
// the screen's default itself.
//
// IT LANDS IN A KEY GROUP, ALWAYS, AND THE KEY GROUP IS NAMED. A default key group exists from the start, so
// the rule is never a wall — `listKeyRings` marks it `is_default` — but the request says which
// key group, every time. The key group is what this operation's permission is ABOUT: "may I make a key
// here" is a question about the container (decision 0632), and a key group left out would turn it
// into "may I make a key anywhere". Whose the key is follows from the key group and is never said
// twice.
//
// A COMPANY KEY IS NEVER ADDED BELOW THE COMPANY'S FLOOR FOR ITS KIND (`AlwaysPrivileged`) OR
// FOR ITS KEY GROUP (`AlwaysPrivilegedRings`), whichever is higher. A Privileged setting below it,
// or none named where there is one, is refused with 422 `privileged_below_company_rule` before
// the value goes anywhere.
//
// A MEMBER'S OWN KEY INSIDE A COMPANY FOLLOWS "PERSONAL KEYS INSIDE THE COMPANY"
// (`PersonalKeysInsideTheCompany`). Where the company keeps none, a key added to a key group held
// for a member is refused with 422 `personal_key_not_allowed` before the value goes anywhere.
// Where the company's rules apply to them, it is held to the floor for its kind, as a company
// key is. A key group of somebody's own has no key group floor: only company key groups are listed.
//
// SHARING TO GUESTS IS A COMPANY KEY'S (`GuestSharing`), `allowed` where it is left out. Named
// for a key that lands in a key group of somebody's own, it is refused with 422
// `guest_sharing_on_a_personal_key` before the value goes anywhere. A `not-allowed` key added
// to a key group an outside person holds is refused with 409 `ring_held_by_an_outside_person`, whose
// `detail` names the share to cut first, before the value goes anywhere (decision 0776).
//
// THE USE WINDOW IS EVERY KEY'S (`UseWindowMinutes`), four hours where it is left out. On a
// company key, named longer than the company's Maximum duration on Check-outs
// (`check_out_maximum_minutes`), it is refused with 422 `use_window_above_maximum_duration`
// before the value goes anywhere. A key that lands in a key group of somebody's own has no Maximum
// duration above it, only the day (ONPLO-1587).
//
// IT WAS OPTIONAL UNTIL 2026-10-02, with `owner_scope` beside it choosing a default key group. That
// landed an hour before the contract could say which object an operation is about, and it
// would have let a caller pick the wider question by leaving a field out.
//
// A DEPLOY ENVIRONMENT'S ENTRY IS MADE THROUGH THAT ENVIRONMENT (ONPLO-4560). A `scope` naming a
// deploy environment is refused with 409 `environment_entry_has_its_own_operation`, whose `detail` names
// `createEnvironmentSecret` and `createEnvironmentVariable`: Flows asks a permission of the
// environment for it, and a contract never makes a permission depend on a value (decision 0831).
// What either makes is still a Keys entry from the first byte (decision 0515). (Until 10 November
// 2026 this said a flow's "Add value" and Keys' "+ New" were the same operation.) A connection's
// credential is the exception by design: Integrations hands it straight to the vault, because
// connections exist before this service does (decision 0512).
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type.
//
// Corresponds with POST /keys (the `CreateKey` operationId).
func (c *Client) CreateKeyWithBody(ctx context.Context, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*http.Response, error) {
	req, err := NewCreateKeyRequestWithBody(c.Server, contentType, body)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	req = req.WithContext(ctx)
	if err := c.applyEditors(ctx, req, reqEditors); err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return c.Client.Do(req)
}

