	// AnswerCheckOutWithBodyWithResponse Approve or refuse somebody's ask for a privileged key
	//
	// APPROVE, OR REFUSE WITH A REASON. Approved, the check-out is `held`: its asker holds the
	// key until `returns_at`. Refused, it is `refused` and stays as a record on the key.
	//
	// NEVER YOUR OWN — `check_out_own_ask`. "An approver can never sign their own ask. The
	// four-eyes rule lives inside the approve verb itself, not in a setting someone can quietly
	// loosen on a busy afternoon." Holding the permission does not change it.
	//
	// ONE HOLDER AT A TIME — `key_checked_out`. An ask cannot be approved while somebody holds
	// the key.
	//
	// ANSWERED ONCE — `check_out_already_answered`. The same answer sent again by the same
	// person changes nothing and answers the check-out as it stands; another answer is refused.
	//
	// APPROVE CHECKS THE ASKER IS STILL HERE. "An ask whose asker is no longer a member of the
	// company cannot be approved, even when news of their leaving has not reached Keys yet.
	// Approve then withdraws the ask as above, and the approver sees why." So an approval of a
	// leaver's ask answers 200 with the check-out `withdrawn`, and nothing is held. A refusal asks
	// nothing about the asker: it holds nothing. When identity cannot be asked who is a member,
	// nothing is approved and the answer is 503, which means ask again.
	//
	// A WITHDRAWN ASK HAS NOTHING LEFT TO ANSWER. Any answer to one changes nothing and answers
	// it as it stands.
	//
	// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
	//
	// Corresponds with PUT /keys/{key_id}/check-outs/{check_out_id}/answer (the `AnswerCheckOut` operationId).
