	// ListPeopleWhoNamedMeWithResponse The people who named you for emergency access
	//
	// THE SAME LIST SEEN FROM THE OTHER SIDE. Each row is one person who named you, with the
	// account environment the row was made in, what you may reach one day, how long you must
	// wait after asking, and where a request stands.
	//
	// NOT GATED. "Asking needs no permission, and neither does seeing who named you. Being named
	// is what lets a person ask, and no role can give or withhold it."
	//
	// THE ROWS THAT NAME YOU AND NO OTHER. A row that names somebody else is absent, so the list
	// never tells you who else a person trusts, or that they trust anybody.
	//
	// A ROW THAT ENDED IS NOT HERE. Once your access to a company has ended, your list "drops
	// every row belonging to that company", so a row that reads `left` on the other side is
	// absent from this one.
	//
	// WHICH ROWS DEPENDS ON WHERE YOU ASK (decision 0764). IN A PERSONAL ENVIRONMENT: every row
	// that names you, "whichever account environment it was made in", so a person named in a
	// company they cannot enter has a place to look. IN A BUSINESS ENVIRONMENT: "the rows made
	// in that environment and no others". It is the same row in both places, under the same id,
	// and `environment_id` says where it was made.
	//
	// A PAGE MAY HOLD FEWER ROWS THAN `limit` AND STILL HAVE A `next_cursor`. In a Personal
	// environment a page is drawn from the identity service's list of the rows that name you
	// (decision 0777), and a row that ended a moment ago is left out of its page. `next_cursor`
	// being `null` is the only end.
	//
	// NOTHING IS ASKED HERE. Asking for access is its own act, `requestEmergencyAccess`.
	//
	// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
