// FetchAttachedKeyWithBodyWithResponse Fetch a key attached to this machine, by the name the machine calls it
//
// A MACHINE FETCHES WHAT IS ATTACHED TO IT, BY NAME, AT RUN TIME (ONPLO-5460, decision 1051). A
// write-only key a person attached to a Machine of Developer Access is fetched by that machine's
// own access key, by the alias the attachment gave it. A Software run is its own machine, so it
// fetches only what is attached to that run.
//
// ASKED UNDER THE ATTACHMENT ITSELF, WITH NO PERMISSION OF ITS OWN (ONPLO-5514). A machine holds
// no role, so no permission could let it fetch. What lets it is the attachment a person made,
// under `keys:key.assign`, and the vault judges it on every fetch. So `security` names the
// credential and no permission, and a person, an agent or an API key that is no machine is
// refused `fetch_needs_a_machine`.
//
// THE VALUE LEAVES LOCKED TO THE MACHINE'S OWN KEY, which only the machine holds (decision 1037,
// point 4). The lock is a JWE in compact form, as the security baseline's §3.2 says: the key the
// machine registered in Developer Access is read from Integrations, and the vault seals the value
// to it. Keys passes the lock on as it came, and never sees the value.
//
// AN ALIAS THIS MACHINE HAS NOTHING ATTACHED UNDER IS THE SHARED 404, and it never says whether
// a key of that name exists anywhere else, or is attached to another machine.
//
// EVERY FETCH IS ONE LINE IN THE AUDIT LOG, about the key, and the vault writes its own.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /attached-keys/fetch (the `FetchAttachedKey` operationId).
