// ListKeysOutsideOnploWithResponse The credentials your governed systems hold that Keys does not
//
// KEYS OUTSIDE ONPLO: "credentials that exist in the systems you already govern but are not
// held here — cloud access keys, service-account keys, deploy keys". A page at a time.
//
// A LENS ON INTEGRATIONS, NEVER A COPY. The rows "are read from the read-only governed systems
// Integrations already holds for this business account environment", through Integrations'
// `ListGovernedSystemCredentials`, at the moment of the answer. Keys stores none of them.
//
// FACTS, AND NEVER A VALUE. Each row is what the system reports: the credential's id and name
// there, its kind, who owns it there, when it was made, when it was last used and whether it
// still works. Its secret half is never fetched, so it cannot be answered: "the value is never
// copied out of the outside system".
//
// OLDEST LAST USE FIRST, THEN OLDEST. One never used comes first, then the one unused longest,
// then the oldest, across every system — the order Integrations answers in, so the first page
// is drawn without reading the rest. "A single cloud organisation commonly holds five figures
// of access keys."
//
// A BUSINESS PAGE. The catalogue gives `keys:unmanaged-key.read` in a business account
// environment alone, and Integrations answers an empty page wherever no governed system is
// connected.
//
// OPENING IT IS A LOOK, AND KEYS RECORDS IT (decision 0675, rule 5). Integrations' read writes
// nothing, so the record of this look is Keys' own event, about the environment and nobody in
// it. A look that was refused saw nothing and leaves none.
//
// NOT YET, EACH WITH ITS REASON:
//   - THE RISK NOTE, AND OLD OR DORMANT. Both are measured against the Key rules' age and
//     dormancy thresholds, and Keys' Key rules hold neither yet (ONPLO-1335). The facts they
//     are measured from are here.
//   - TRACK HERE AND LEAVE IT. The catalogue states no permission for either, so until both
//     exist every credential the systems hold is a row here, and none is taken away.
//   - MORE THAN ONE SOURCE AT ONCE. One governed system, or every one; a list of several is a
//     new parameter beside this one when the page needs it.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with GET /keys-outside-onplo (the `ListKeysOutsideOnplo` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) ListKeysOutsideOnploWithResponse(ctx context.Context, params *ListKeysOutsideOnploParams, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*ListKeysOutsideOnploResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.ListKeysOutsideOnplo(ctx, params, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseListKeysOutsideOnploResponse(rsp)
}

// TrashKeyWithResponse Move a key to the Trash
//
// TRASH IS THE ONLY DELETE. The key waits there for the account environment's own Trash
// keep-time — thirty days by default, set once in Storage for everything — and then the
// deletion orchestrator erases it for real and issues the receipt. Nothing here erases.
//
// A KEY STILL ATTACHED ANYWHERE IS REFUSED, and `detail` names what uses it, because stopping
// a flow by deleting its key is damage nobody chose. So is a key a connection uses as its
// credential.
//
// IT ANSWERS THE KEY, with `trashed_at` set, rather than nothing: the row is still there, in
// the Trash, and a client shows it.
