// TurnKeyPrivilegedOffWithResponse Turn a key's Privileged setting off
//
// "TURNING IT OFF is a permission of its own, Turn a key's Privileged setting off, because
// removing a safeguard needs more than adding one. Turning it on stays part of changing a key,
// and a company floor that forces it on still wins." So a key set to Ask before use or
// Check-out goes to `off` here and nowhere else: `updateKey` refuses a change naming `off`
// for a key that stands above it. Moving between Ask before use and Check-out stays a change.
//
// A COMPANY FLOOR STILL WINS. A key the company's rules keep at Ask before use or Check-out,
// for its kind (`AlwaysPrivileged`) or its key group (`AlwaysPrivilegedRings`), is refused with
// 409 `privileged_kept_by_company_rule`, and nothing changes. A member's own key inside a
// company is held to the floor for its kind while the company's rules apply to personal keys
// (`PersonalKeysInsideTheCompany`).
//
// A KEY ALREADY `off` CHANGES NOTHING and is answered as it is — a look (decision 0685), not
// a refusal. A key Keys holds no row for is `off` already.
//
// ITS CHECK-OUTS STAY AS THEY ARE, as they do through any change of the setting: an ask still
// waits for its answer, and a holder keeps the key until it is handed back or its time is up.
// A new ask is refused, because the key is no longer set to Check-out.
//
// ASKED ACROSS THE ACCOUNT ENVIRONMENT, NEVER OF THE ONE KEY: the catalogue grants
// `keys:key-privilege.delete` there and on no single object, so this operation names none.
//
// THE AUDIT RECORD NAMES `privileged` among what changed.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with DELETE /keys/{key_id}/privileged (the `TurnKeyPrivilegedOff` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) TurnKeyPrivilegedOffWithResponse(ctx context.Context, keyId KeyId, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*TurnKeyPrivilegedOffResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.TurnKeyPrivilegedOff(ctx, keyId, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseTurnKeyPrivilegedOffResponse(rsp)
}

// PromoteKeyIntoCompanyRingWithBodyWithResponse Promote a personal key into a company key group
//
// "Promote into a company key group" — the publish verb in its only Keys sense. A key in your
// personal corner of a company becomes one of the company's keys, in the key group you name. It
// never leaves the company. THERE IS NO WAY BACK: a promoted key is company data, and it never
// moves into a person's corner again (decision 0807, rule 6).
//
// ASKED OF BOTH HALVES IN ONE QUESTION (decision 0632, rule 6): `keys:key.publish` on the key,
// and write on the key group it lands in, which is `keys:key-ring.change` — "move keys in and out of
// it". No approval is added: the key group's write permission is already somebody's decision about
// who may place keys there (decision 0807, rule 3).
//
// WHAT THE KEY CARRIES ACROSS: its value, its previous values, write-only, Privileged, its
// attachments and its record. WHAT IT DOES NOT: the grants placed on it in the corner. From
// now on, who may see and use it is the key group's answer, so each of those grants is ended
// before the key moves (rule 5). A grant on the whole environment is not the corner's and
// stays.
//
// ITS PREVIOUS VALUES ARE CUT TO WHAT THE COMPANY KEEPS, in the move itself: the key group's rules
// "apply at once and only ever tighten" (rule 4). The oldest beyond the company's count — its
// Key rules' number, ten where it set none — leave use when the key moves, and are set aside
// until the deletion service asks for them to be erased. Until 2026-10-16 they stayed until the
// key's next rotation (ONPLO-2582).
//
// A STEWARD IS NAMED, the promoter when `steward` is left out. Someone who is not a person
// names one.
//
// IT BECOMES SHAREABLE TO GUESTS (`GuestSharing`, `allowed`), the default every company key
// starts at, and its key group's own rule still has its say. The audit record names
// `guest_sharing` among what changed.
//
// WHAT IS NOT DONE YET, said so nobody reads it as done:
//   - A KEY TAKES THE COMPANY'S FLOOR IN THE MOVE, if it stood below it: "a key group that is
//     always privileged makes the key privileged" (`AlwaysPrivilegedRings`), and a kind's
//     floor (`AlwaysPrivileged`) is that rule for kinds; the higher of the two where both
//     stand. The audit record names `privileged` among what changed;
//   - the key's History line arrives with the History (ONPLO-1654). The audit record is
//     written now: one, naming the promoter, the corner's key group and the company's.
//
// WHAT IT WILL DO IS ASKED FIRST with `previewKeyPromotion`, which changes nothing: who loses
// access, how many previous values go, and the Privileged setting the key lands with.
//
// SENT AGAIN AFTER IT WORKED, it is refused with `key_already_in_a_company_ring`: the key is
// the company's now, and the answer to the first request was the promoted key.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /keys/{key_id}/promotion (the `PromoteKeyIntoCompanyRing` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) PromoteKeyIntoCompanyRingWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, keyId KeyId, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*PromoteKeyIntoCompanyRingResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.PromoteKeyIntoCompanyRingWithBody(ctx, keyId, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParsePromoteKeyIntoCompanyRingResponse(rsp)
}

