// RestoreKeyFromTrashWithResponse Bring a key back from the Trash
//
// ONE CLICK, AND IT IS BACK WHERE IT WAS. "Restore, one click, puts it back where it was" —
// its key group, its name, everything that may use it may use it again. The Trash keep-time stops
// counting for it.
//
// ITS OWN PERMISSION (decision 0651), never the one that put it in the Trash.
//
// A KEY THAT IS NOT IN THE TRASH IS ANSWERED AS IT IS. Restoring it changed nothing and showed
// it, which is a look (decision 0685) — not a refusal, so a call sent twice does not fail
// the second time.
//
// THE OBJECT IS THE KEY. A key in the Trash is still in its key group, so the permission is asked of
// the key in the path, as moving it to the Trash is.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /keys/{key_id}/restore (the `RestoreKeyFromTrash` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) RestoreKeyFromTrashWithResponse(ctx context.Context, keyId KeyId, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*RestoreKeyFromTrashResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.RestoreKeyFromTrash(ctx, keyId, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseRestoreKeyFromTrashResponse(rsp)
}

// RevealKeyValueWithResponse Reveal a readable key's value
//
// THE ONE OPERATION THAT RETURNS A VALUE, and its own right: `keys:key-value.read` is granted
// and recorded on its own, never implied by seeing the key. Every reveal is one line in the
// audit log.
//
// A POST AND NOT A GET, because it is an act with a record and never a thing to cache, link
// to or leave in a history.
//
// A WRITE-ONLY ENTRY REVEALS NOTHING, TO ANYBODY — `key_write_only`. Not a permission that is
// missing: nothing can read it, the Onplo team included, so nothing can grant it.
//
// IT ASKS YOU TO PROVE IT IS YOU AGAIN when your last passkey proof is older than the
// freshness window — five minutes by default, set in Keys → Settings → Locking & reveal. One
// proof covers the whole window. Refused `passkey_proof_required` until you do. A company's Key
// rules may hold every member to a shorter window, `reveal_proof_floor_minutes`, and the lower
// of the two counts. Until Keys stores the window a person sets (ONPLO-1335), a person's is five
// minutes for everybody.
//
// WHAT CLEARS THAT REFUSAL IS TWO CALLS ON THE IDENTITY API, and then this one again:
// `startPasskeyProof` (`POST /passkey-proofs`) hands you the question a passkey answers, and
// `completePasskeyProof` (`POST /passkey-proofs/{passkey_proof_id}/completion`) brings the
// answer back (decision 0636). Completing one moves the moment THIS session last proved
// itself and changes nothing else — it widens no scope and it is not a second sign-in. A
// proof made in another session, on another device, does not count here.
//
// WHO HOLDS WHAT: identity says WHEN you last proved it, Keys holds HOW LONG that lasts. So
// the window is never sent to identity and never answered by it, and a key rule that
// shortens it changes nothing outside this app.
//
// A PRIVILEGED KEY IS REVEALED ONLY INSIDE WHAT WAS APPROVED. One set to Ask before use needs
// an ask of yours on it, approved, whose window has not ended — `key_use_not_approved`. One
// set to Check-out needs a check-out of yours on it that holds the key now —
// `key_not_checked_out_to_you`. Neither refusal says whether anybody else holds one.
//
// A REVEAL FREEZE REFUSES IT — `reveal_frozen` — and `detail` carries the reason in words. It
// is the one power the Onplo team holds over customer secrets (decision 0538): two Platform
// Admins together may mask every readable value platform-wide while a flaw in the reveal path
// is fixed. It reads no value, changes none, expires by itself, and never stops a USE.
//
// A DEVICE DEVICES SAID WAS WIPED IS REFUSED — `device_wiped` — before anything else about the
// key is asked, a passkey proof included, since no proof would clear it. "The device's Keys
// clients simply stop being served." Your session is not ended, because ending one is Account's.
// A call from a credential no device is known for is not refused for this.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /keys/{key_id}/reveal (the `RevealKeyValue` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) RevealKeyValueWithResponse(ctx context.Context, keyId KeyId, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*RevealKeyValueResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.RevealKeyValue(ctx, keyId, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseRevealKeyValueResponse(rsp)
}

// MoveKeyToRingWithBodyWithResponse Move a key into another key group
//
// Its own operation, and gated on the KEY GROUP rather than on the key: the catalogue puts "move
// keys in and out of it" on `keys:key-ring.change`, because which key group a key is in decides
// who reaches it. A field on `updateKey` would have put that behind the wrong permission.
//
// EVERY KEY IS IN EXACTLY ONE KEY GROUP, so this moves it and never copies it, and there is no way
// to take a key out of every key group.
//
// A KEY GROUP IN THE OTHER OWNER SCOPE IS REFUSED. Moving a personal key into a company key group is a
// PROMOTION — a different act with its own permission and its own record
// (`promoteKeyIntoCompanyRing`) — and it is not this operation.
//
// A NOT-SHAREABLE KEY IS NOT MOVED INTO A KEY GROUP AN OUTSIDE PERSON HOLDS (decision 0776): a
// company key whose Sharing to guests is `not-allowed` is refused with 409
// `ring_held_by_an_outside_person`, whose `detail` names the share to cut first, and nothing
// moves.
//
// A COMPANY KEY MOVED INTO A KEY GROUP THE COMPANY KEEPS ALWAYS PRIVILEGED TAKES THAT KEY GROUP'S FLOOR,
// if it stood below it (`AlwaysPrivilegedRings`), as a promoted key does: the key group's rules
// apply at once and only tighten. The move is the vault's change and its event; the raised
// setting is Keys', and leaves one audit event of its own, the key its target, naming
// `privileged`. A key moved out of such a key group keeps its setting: a rule never loosens a key.
//
// BOTH KEY GROUPS ARE ASKED ABOUT, IN ONE QUESTION (decision 0632, rule 6). The object is the KEY,
// so one half asks whether you may change the key group it is leaving — which is what stops
// somebody lifting a key out of a key group they do not manage into one they do. The other half
// asks the same permission of the key group it lands in, named by `key_ring_id` — which is what
// stops somebody dropping a key into a key group whose members they do not decide. The move is
// allowed only when both are; refused, it says `permission_missing` once, whichever half it
// was. (Until ONPLO-932 the contract could name one object, and only the first half was
// asked.)
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with PUT /keys/{key_id}/ring (the `MoveKeyToRing` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) MoveKeyToRingWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, keyId KeyId, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*MoveKeyToRingResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.MoveKeyToRingWithBody(ctx, keyId, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseMoveKeyToRingResponse(rsp)
}

